<gpx version="1.1" creator="Claude Bruce Trail planner" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
<metadata><name>Day 80 - Cape Chin Meadows, Pat Daunt</name></metadata>
<wpt lat="45.111658" lon="-81.314430"><name>P Start/finish parking</name><desc>Roadside parking along the eastern shoulder of Cape Chin North Rd where the side trail…. Free</desc><sym>Parking Area</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.113220" lon="-81.316100"><name>P2 alternative parking</name><desc>Roadside parking along the shoulders of Cape Chin North Rd. Free. Joins the route near km 10.9 (0 m walk).</desc><sym>Parking Area</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.107146" lon="-81.290130"><name>J1</name><desc>J1: Turn right onto the blue-blazed Pat Daunt Side Trail, heading east. (the blue-blazed Cape Chin Meadows Side Trail goes off ahead.)</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.110713" lon="-81.285559"><name>J2</name><desc>J2: Turnaround point. The side trail meets the white-blazed main Bruce Trail here. Turn around and retrace your steps.</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.114948" lon="-81.284105"><name>J3</name><desc>J3: Turn left onto the white-blazed main Bruce Trail, heading north-west. (the white-blazed main Bruce Trail goes off on your right.)</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.117802" lon="-81.304589"><name>J4</name><desc>J4: Turn left onto the blue-blazed Cape Chin Meadows Side Trail, heading south-west. (the white-blazed main Bruce Trail goes off ahead.)</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.117437" lon="-81.313041"><name>J5</name><desc>J5: Junction. Continue straight to stay on the Cape Chin Meadows Side Trail, heading west. (the blue-blazed Bard Side Trail goes off on your right.)</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="45.113342" lon="-81.316032"><name>J6</name><desc>J6: Junction. Turn sharp left to stay on the Cape Chin Meadows Side Trail, heading south-east. (the blue-blazed Otter Lake Side Trail goes off ahead.) There is trailhead parking here (Roadside parking along the shoulders of Cape Chin North Rd). The next 0.2 km follows a road shoulder; keep the dog close.</desc><sym>Waypoint</sym></wpt>
